1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,000
From the thick of battle to the edge of disaster,

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:11,000
experience the impossible from the safety of your virtual driver's seat.

3
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Simulators date back to the 1930s, when they were invented as a cheap way to learn how

4
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:49,400
to fly.

5
00:00:49,401 --> 00:00:57,320
Today, they are everywhere, from cutting-edge military hardware to the latest attraction

6
00:00:57,321 --> 00:01:01,600
at the local arcade.

7
00:01:01,601 --> 00:01:07,640
At the United Airlines Flight Center in Denver, Colorado, 36 mammoth simulators provide a

8
00:01:07,641 --> 00:01:14,320
flying experience so realistic it's easy to forget you're still on the ground.

9
00:01:14,321 --> 00:01:19,320
Wind shear, wind shear, wind shear.

10
00:01:19,321 --> 00:01:25,320
Landing gear is down here.

11
00:01:25,321 --> 00:01:27,160
Let's take the flaps to 20.

12
00:01:28,120 --> 00:01:29,360
124 knots.

13
00:01:29,540 --> 00:01:30,420
2,000 down.

14
00:01:31,520 --> 00:01:32,380
1,000 feet.

15
00:01:33,320 --> 00:01:35,720
It is very, very much like flying an airplane.

16
00:01:36,320 --> 00:01:39,940
I'd say on a scale of 100, about 99.9.

17
00:01:40,340 --> 00:01:41,820
It's amazing how good they are.

18
00:01:42,440 --> 00:01:44,560
When you experience a ride in the simulator,

19
00:01:44,980 --> 00:01:46,640
you feel like you're in an airplane,

20
00:01:46,780 --> 00:01:48,680
and you really can't distinguish the difference.

21
00:01:48,900 --> 00:01:50,520
We can do things in the simulator

22
00:01:50,521 --> 00:01:52,300
that we'd never do in an airplane.

23
00:01:52,301 --> 00:01:54,319
We don't like how it occurred.

24
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:59,300
We simply put the simulator back at the end of the runway and do it again.

25
00:01:59,301 --> 00:02:04,300
Today, most pilots do all their training on a simulator.

26
00:02:04,301 --> 00:02:12,300
We can train crews now from their first day on the airplane through their final licensing and certification,

27
00:02:12,301 --> 00:02:16,300
such that their first flight is a revenue flight.

28
00:02:16,301 --> 00:02:21,700
flight.

29
00:02:21,701 --> 00:02:27,300
The history of this remarkable device dates back to the early days of flight itself.

30
00:02:27,301 --> 00:02:32,160
During the First World War, both the French and British came up with several curious devices

31
00:02:32,161 --> 00:02:38,440
to train pilots, including the wingless penguin that taxied but never took off, and a half

32
00:02:38,441 --> 00:02:42,180
barrel with wings called the Antoinette.

33
00:02:42,181 --> 00:02:47,180
The Ruggles Orientator was used by the French for testing pilots for the right stuff.

34
00:02:50,180 --> 00:02:54,180
In the US, not even these devices were available.

35
00:02:54,181 --> 00:03:00,180
Lessons in a real plane cost $50 an hour for what was often no more than a harrowing joyride.

36
00:03:02,180 --> 00:03:08,180
Ed Link, a young pilot from Binghampton, New York, decided there had to be a better way.

37
00:03:08,181 --> 00:03:16,680
Ed was a pilot, a barnstormer, back in the days when people wing walked.

38
00:03:16,681 --> 00:03:21,800
His father was an inventor and producer of theater organs.

39
00:03:21,801 --> 00:03:25,720
And these were the big organs that were used in silent movie theaters.

40
00:03:25,721 --> 00:03:33,360
And Ed found himself with a lot of inventory of old bellows and electro-mechanical devices

41
00:03:33,361 --> 00:03:36,000
from his father's organ factory.

42
00:03:36,001 --> 00:03:40,000
and actually used a lot of that technology to put together his first trainer.

43
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:46,000
In 1929, Link patented his first pilot trainer.

44
00:03:46,001 --> 00:03:52,000
It was a cockpit mounted on top of four bellows that expanded and contracted to provide motion.

45
00:03:52,001 --> 00:03:56,000
It could bank, tilt and even spin around.

46
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:05,000
It was intended to be a training device, a cheap way to learn how to fly.

47
00:04:05,001 --> 00:04:13,000
But to Link's dismay, the only buyers were amusement parks, where his pilot trainer became just another novelty ride.

48
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:18,000
Determined, Link set up his own flight school.

49
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:23,000
For a flat fee of $85, he'd teach you how to fly, guaranteed.

50
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:29,000
Unfortunately, the Great Depression was in full swing, and even $85 was a lot of money.

51
00:04:29,001 --> 00:04:29,701
of money.

52
00:04:29,702 --> 00:04:36,360
Then in 1934 an antitrust decision forced commercial aeroplane makers out of the air mail

53
00:04:36,361 --> 00:04:40,840
business and the job of flying the mail was handed over to the Army Air Corps.

54
00:04:41,720 --> 00:04:45,560
In their first few days of operation five planes crashed.

55
00:04:52,360 --> 00:04:55,800
They were having a terrible time trying to deliver the mail and people were flying in

56
00:04:55,801 --> 00:04:58,960
in the mountains and getting lost in storms.

57
00:04:58,961 --> 00:05:03,080
In the 30s, most pilots flew by the seat of their pants,

58
00:05:03,081 --> 00:05:06,640
depending on their senses rather than their instruments.

59
00:05:06,641 --> 00:05:09,920
This required being able to at least see the horizon.

60
00:05:09,921 --> 00:05:13,360
Darkness or storm clouds created an unusual and very

61
00:05:13,361 --> 00:05:16,700
dangerous problem.

62
00:05:16,701 --> 00:05:18,460
The problem with that is something we call

63
00:05:18,461 --> 00:05:19,700
spatial disorientation.

64
00:05:19,701 --> 00:05:24,880
You have inter-ear sensations that can get very confused

65
00:05:24,881 --> 00:05:29,520
when you lose a natural horizon and you can end up thinking you are climbing, diving,

66
00:05:29,680 --> 00:05:31,960
turning, and in fact that is not what you're doing at all.

67
00:05:33,080 --> 00:05:37,980
Suddenly, there was an urgent need to teach experienced pilots how to fly by instruments,

68
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:40,280
and Ed Link seized the opportunity.

69
00:05:41,480 --> 00:05:46,380
He installed a compass and a turn and bank indicator into his pilot trainers,

70
00:05:46,381 --> 00:05:50,180
whose motion caused them to respond just as they would in flight.

71
00:05:50,181 --> 00:05:57,000
For extra measure, he added a hood so the pilot would be forced to fly blind.

72
00:05:57,900 --> 00:06:01,820
The Army was interested, but they wanted to see the trainer in action.

73
00:06:02,660 --> 00:06:06,560
They had set up a demonstration out down in Fort Monmouth, New Jersey.

74
00:06:07,340 --> 00:06:10,380
And it was bad weather. They said he wasn't going to be able to arrive.

75
00:06:10,940 --> 00:06:17,260
And lo and behold, perfect salesman, he landed his airplane and demonstrated to the Army Air Corps

76
00:06:17,261 --> 00:06:21,300
and got the first order for, as I recall, six trainers at that time.

77
00:06:22,300 --> 00:06:26,760
By the end of 1934, the first commercially successful flight simulator

78
00:06:26,761 --> 00:06:30,760
went into production at Link's small plant at Binghampton, New York.

79
00:06:31,460 --> 00:06:34,540
Its acceptance by pilots, however, was another matter.

80
00:06:36,740 --> 00:06:40,120
Pilots didn't really care for those. They considered them torture boxes.

81
00:06:40,540 --> 00:06:43,260
They weren't air-conditioned. They had a small vent fan.

82
00:06:43,261 --> 00:06:47,240
But I guarantee you that at the end of a simulator training period in those days,

83
00:06:47,260 --> 00:06:49,900
you came out physically exhausted.

84
00:06:51,820 --> 00:06:53,780
With the bombing of Pearl Harbor

85
00:06:53,781 --> 00:06:56,560
and the entry of the U.S. into the Second World War,

86
00:06:56,920 --> 00:07:00,340
a massive and immediate demand for trained pilots was created.

87
00:07:06,480 --> 00:07:08,440
Nearly half a million civilians

88
00:07:08,441 --> 00:07:11,200
got their first taste of flying in link simulators,

89
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:14,020
which they affectionately called blue boxes.

90
00:07:14,021 --> 00:07:19,280
There were hundreds and hundreds produced of the so-called blue box.

91
00:07:19,640 --> 00:07:22,920
Thousands of pilots were trained in link boxes.

92
00:07:24,040 --> 00:07:27,140
Other tasks called for more specialized skills.

93
00:07:28,080 --> 00:07:33,980
Because bomber crews flew many of their missions at night, they needed to learn how to navigate by the stars.

94
00:07:34,120 --> 00:07:37,280
They didn't have all the electronic ground positioning systems.

95
00:07:37,400 --> 00:07:39,560
They were still using celestial navigation.

96
00:07:39,561 --> 00:07:51,820
So the company was put under contract to design and build a very, very sophisticated, very accurate simulation of the celestial sphere.

97
00:07:53,500 --> 00:08:02,480
With its rotating ceiling of stars, the celestial navigation trainer allowed an entire bomber crew to navigate and fly a simulated mission.

98
00:08:02,481 --> 00:08:10,140
It was a real phenomenon in its time, optically correct, and it was quite an achievement.

99
00:08:12,360 --> 00:08:16,260
During the war, the Navy began developing its own simulators,

100
00:08:16,760 --> 00:08:20,940
establishing the special devices section under Commander Louis de Flores.

101
00:08:21,980 --> 00:08:29,580
Louis de Flores was looking for means of training crews in a specific aircraft

102
00:08:29,581 --> 00:08:34,780
that would go beyond the instrument flight trainer, which had a limited envelope of operation.

103
00:08:35,940 --> 00:08:43,740
And De Flores took visits to England, in which he saw a trainer there, developed by the British, called the Siloth trainer.

104
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:52,580
The Siloth inspired De Flores to develop a trainer for another specific plane, the PBM, that was used to hunt for U-boats.

105
00:08:53,380 --> 00:08:58,400
Because the Navy's fleet of PBMs were busy searching for subs, few could be spared for training.

106
00:08:59,580 --> 00:09:09,080
Working with Bell Labs, De Flores group built a simulator radically different from Link's

107
00:09:09,081 --> 00:09:09,781
blue box.

108
00:09:09,782 --> 00:09:15,060
Instead of a few simple instruments, it included virtually every system aboard

109
00:09:15,061 --> 00:09:18,860
the aircraft, all interconnected by an analog computer.

110
00:09:18,861 --> 00:09:23,220
In those days, computer technology was just emerging.

111
00:09:23,221 --> 00:09:26,100
In fact, many of the computers were

112
00:09:26,101 --> 00:09:30,980
relegated to research laboratories. They hadn't found their way into commercial or

113
00:09:30,981 --> 00:09:36,220
industrial applications yet. But the technology was there. It had to be applied.

114
00:09:36,221 --> 00:09:39,620
Largely due to its increased size,

115
00:09:39,621 --> 00:09:44,220
the PBM trainer was stationary. It didn't tilt or spin like the old link

116
00:09:44,221 --> 00:09:44,921
trainers,

117
00:09:44,922 --> 00:09:47,480
but it did have one major improvement.

118
00:09:47,481 --> 00:09:52,040
It could break down.

119
00:09:52,041 --> 00:09:57,040
The instructor had a control board full of emergencies he could trigger with the flick of a switch.

120
00:09:57,041 --> 00:10:02,040
You could fail each one of the fuel pumps, you could fail each one of the transfer valves.

121
00:10:02,041 --> 00:10:07,040
If there were gauges that told about fuel quantity, you could fail the gauges,

122
00:10:07,041 --> 00:10:11,040
so that they thought they had no fuel but they actually had fuel.

123
00:10:11,041 --> 00:10:16,040
So what this did is take all of the material they'd learned in classrooms about the airplane

124
00:10:16,041 --> 00:10:21,040
and about their crew functions and put them in an actual situation where they could do it.

125
00:10:21,041 --> 00:10:22,218
they could do it.

126
00:10:22,219 --> 00:10:26,860
They could see it, they could hear it, they could feel it, and they

127
00:10:26,861 --> 00:10:30,086
would respond to the situation that was occurring.

128
00:10:30,087 --> 00:10:32,860
So it gave them the reality of being there.

129
00:10:32,861 --> 00:10:38,036
In fact, there are stories about people who were carried away.

130
00:10:38,037 --> 00:10:40,040
They were so involved in

131
00:10:40,041 --> 00:10:44,800
the emergencies that existed in a simulator, they said, let's abandon the airplane.

132
00:10:44,801 --> 00:10:51,560
It was this ability to confront pilots with controlled malfunctions

133
00:10:51,561 --> 00:10:55,880
that was to give simulators a permanent place in aviation history.

134
00:11:14,800 --> 00:11:20,040
Here

135
00:11:20,041 --> 00:11:25,280
Here

136
00:11:25,281 --> 00:11:26,840
VH1.

137
00:11:27,280 --> 00:11:28,020
Panoapola.

138
00:11:28,300 --> 00:11:29,000
Music.

139
00:11:29,300 --> 00:11:30,120
Apoquitika.

140
00:11:30,240 --> 00:11:30,940
St. Nova.

141
00:12:11,057 --> 00:12:18,060
The end of the Second World War saw the arrival of a new era of larger, faster commercial airlines.

142
00:12:18,061 --> 00:12:23,060
To train pilots on new model passenger planes like the jet-propelled DC-8,

143
00:12:23,061 --> 00:12:28,060
the airline industry began using simulators designed for those specific planes.

144
00:12:30,060 --> 00:12:33,060
They were a great leap forward, no question about that,

145
00:12:33,061 --> 00:12:36,060
because they did model the exact cockpits of different aircraft.

146
00:12:36,061 --> 00:12:39,060
More of the actual aircraft instrumentation was present.

147
00:12:39,061 --> 00:12:43,060
With that instrumentation, you now knew what your engines were doing,

148
00:12:43,061 --> 00:12:46,060
what your different systems of the airplane were doing,

149
00:12:46,061 --> 00:12:50,060
and you could really expand the area that you were able to train in

150
00:12:50,061 --> 00:12:52,640
more than just hand flying the airplane.

151
00:12:54,400 --> 00:12:57,800
Their one major drawback was that they flew blind.

152
00:13:00,100 --> 00:13:02,760
We didn't have the visual systems that we have now.

153
00:13:03,140 --> 00:13:04,560
They had black windows, really.

154
00:13:04,840 --> 00:13:09,240
One of the first things that was tried was a point of light on a slit

155
00:13:09,241 --> 00:13:12,040
to represent a runway on a screen.

156
00:13:12,041 --> 00:13:15,040
When television came about, it was illogical.

157
00:13:15,041 --> 00:13:19,040
Let's use television technology to do our visual systems.

158
00:13:20,040 --> 00:13:23,040
They attached a TV camera to a moving track

159
00:13:23,041 --> 00:13:26,040
that was suspended above a huge model landscape.

160
00:13:26,041 --> 00:13:29,040
Guided by the simulator's flight controls,

161
00:13:29,041 --> 00:13:33,040
the camera sent pictures to a monitor mounted outside the cockpit.

162
00:13:33,041 --> 00:13:38,040
You'd have a model of the real world, little trees, little houses.

163
00:13:38,041 --> 00:13:40,040
They were very massive devices.

164
00:13:40,041 --> 00:13:42,040
They had to have gantries.

165
00:13:42,041 --> 00:13:45,040
They had to move those gantries around precisely.

166
00:13:45,041 --> 00:13:47,040
It didn't work too well.

167
00:13:47,041 --> 00:13:50,040
First of all, it had a very narrow field of view.

168
00:13:50,041 --> 00:13:53,040
Pilots have a wide field of view.

169
00:13:53,041 --> 00:13:56,040
So when you restrict their field of view,

170
00:13:56,041 --> 00:13:59,040
you're not giving them the same characteristics

171
00:13:59,041 --> 00:14:02,040
they'd have when they were flying the airplanes.

172
00:14:02,041 --> 00:14:04,040
At McGuire Air Force Base, the newest thing

173
00:14:04,041 --> 00:14:07,040
in flight simulators goes operational.

174
00:14:07,041 --> 00:14:14,040
It's the first simulator to utilize a digital computer allowing automatic programming of flight situations.

175
00:14:14,041 --> 00:14:21,040
In the early 60s, simulators were being built that replaced analog computers with digital ones.

176
00:14:21,041 --> 00:14:30,040
The problem with analog computers was that they were all hardware, even small changes entailed changing parts inside the computer itself.

177
00:14:30,041 --> 00:14:33,078
It was just like designing an airplane.

178
00:14:33,079 --> 00:14:44,530
Now if you found out later on that what you had designed was incorrect, you had to change the design of the computer, which was not a trivial task.

179
00:14:44,531 --> 00:14:46,400
It took time and effort.

180
00:14:46,401 --> 00:14:57,400
With digital computers, modelling how a specific plane behaved became a software problem, saving time, expense and vastly improving precision and accuracy.

181
00:14:57,401 --> 00:15:06,400
Digital computers also have the power to simulate motion using a hydraulic base that was far more realistic than the old link trainers.

182
00:15:06,401 --> 00:15:13,400
The computer gave us the ability to induce the senses that you would feel in the airplane,

183
00:15:13,401 --> 00:15:16,400
the g-forces, acceleration, deceleration.

184
00:15:16,401 --> 00:15:19,400
All of those things now were able to be replicated.

185
00:15:19,401 --> 00:15:22,400
We weren't fearful of inducing the wrong sensation.

186
00:15:22,401 --> 00:15:25,400
The sensation would match what the airplane was doing.

187
00:15:28,400 --> 00:15:33,400
But far and away the most advanced simulator of the 1960s

188
00:15:33,401 --> 00:15:37,400
was the one built by the Link Corporation for the Apollo Moon mission.

189
00:15:38,600 --> 00:15:46,520
The Apollo mission simulator had to simulate every aspect of life on board an Apollo.

190
00:15:46,521 --> 00:15:52,360
It would have been difficult to design the simulator if all of the design data had already been complete and available

191
00:15:53,080 --> 00:15:56,299
to be handed over to the simulator designers.

192
00:15:56,300 --> 00:15:59,160
But when the design is evolving in time,

193
00:15:59,161 --> 00:16:03,240
We were trying to design a really, really complex simulator.

194
00:16:03,780 --> 00:16:05,760
The problems become monumental.

195
00:16:06,960 --> 00:16:10,080
All of those darn on-board computers had to be simulated.

196
00:16:10,460 --> 00:16:14,860
And worst of all, all of the external visual scene had to be simulated.

197
00:16:17,280 --> 00:16:20,800
Video images of enormous models of the Earth and the Moon

198
00:16:20,801 --> 00:16:24,920
were transmitted to monitors positioned outside the space capsule's windows.

199
00:16:24,921 --> 00:16:28,080
It was an exact replica of the spacecraft,

200
00:16:28,081 --> 00:16:33,220
including all the stowage, we would have all of the maps, all of the clothing,

201
00:16:33,560 --> 00:16:37,120
everything you had in flight was simulated in its location.

202
00:16:37,880 --> 00:16:42,880
Every aspect of the mission, including landing and walking on the moon, was simulated.

203
00:16:43,860 --> 00:16:46,840
For the major simulations, everybody was on station

204
00:16:46,841 --> 00:16:50,740
because everyone was learning their role, how to communicate.

205
00:16:50,880 --> 00:16:53,500
One of the most difficult things when you have this many people

206
00:16:53,501 --> 00:16:56,520
right around the world all trying to work on one problem,

207
00:16:56,521 --> 00:17:01,180
So you have to learn how to exchange information quickly and succinctly.

208
00:17:02,180 --> 00:17:04,700
When asked what it was like to go to the moon,

209
00:17:05,220 --> 00:17:08,980
Neil Armstrong is said to have replied, it was just like the simulator.

210
00:17:08,981 --> 00:17:10,620
I'm going to step off the lam now.

211
00:17:11,600 --> 00:17:16,280
That's one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind.

212
00:17:17,840 --> 00:17:24,340
But one of the simulator's greatest moments came with the events that began on April the 13th, 1970.

213
00:17:24,341 --> 00:17:31,340
Just as Apollo 13 was about to enter lunar orbit, an onboard oxygen tank exploded.

214
00:17:31,341 --> 00:17:34,340
Okay, Houston, we've had a problem here.

215
00:17:34,341 --> 00:17:36,340
Houston, we've had a problem.

216
00:17:36,341 --> 00:17:38,340
The bus waterfall.

217
00:17:38,341 --> 00:17:40,340
We may have had an instrumentation problem, Flight.

218
00:17:40,341 --> 00:17:41,340
Roger.

219
00:17:41,341 --> 00:17:47,340
And we had a pretty large bang associated with the caution and warning there.

220
00:17:47,341 --> 00:17:52,340
Astronaut Ken Mattingly had been pulled from the Apollo 13 crew just days before launch

221
00:17:52,341 --> 00:17:55,340
because of concern over his exposure to measles.

222
00:17:55,341 --> 00:17:58,340
He was in mission control when the disaster occurred.

223
00:18:00,340 --> 00:18:04,340
Jim reported that there's stuff pouring out of the side of the spacecraft.

224
00:18:04,341 --> 00:18:08,340
We don't know what happened. We do know we're losing oxygen.

225
00:18:08,341 --> 00:18:13,340
We know that it's escaping, the pressures in the tanks are all dropping precipitously,

226
00:18:13,341 --> 00:18:16,340
The electrical power is starting to fall off the line.

227
00:18:16,341 --> 00:18:21,340
And what caused it wasn't as important as what do you do now.

228
00:18:22,340 --> 00:18:24,340
They were fresh out of ideas.

229
00:18:24,341 --> 00:18:29,340
They needed to get the crew out of the command module and into the lunar module.

230
00:18:30,340 --> 00:18:34,340
About that time somebody remembered an earlier simulation.

231
00:18:36,340 --> 00:18:41,340
Throughout the entire crisis, the simulator became a testbed for ideas and solutions.

232
00:18:41,341 --> 00:18:46,140
and solutions. We've tried to simulate virtually everything that we've had the

233
00:18:46,141 --> 00:18:52,140
crew to do that is non-normal. Quickly the astronauts moved into the

234
00:18:52,141 --> 00:18:58,020
relative safety of the lunar module. They moved people into there and the idea

235
00:18:58,021 --> 00:19:01,920
was to get the electrical loads off the command module so that the batteries

