00:38:51,941 --> 00:38:55,560
problem I knew that there was a problem and I had to take care of it it was

461
00:38:55,561 --> 00:39:00,880
stressful. You can stress them or you can test them, but you can match them with

462
00:39:00,881 --> 00:39:04,800
their ability and the patient's level of illness, which is something that we can

463
00:39:04,801 --> 00:39:10,380
never do in the OR. The military now uses patient simulators to keep medics

464
00:39:10,381 --> 00:39:15,300
proficient in the latest techniques for treating traumatic injuries. We've got to

465
00:39:15,301 --> 00:39:18,420
have realism in our training and I think this program is going to also assure

466
00:39:18,421 --> 00:39:23,800
that we have prepared men and women ready to go into combat.

467
00:39:23,801 --> 00:39:30,800
By the late 80s, the US military was using simulators in almost every area of operation.

468
00:39:30,801 --> 00:39:32,800
But one challenge remained.

469
00:39:32,801 --> 00:39:36,800
How to bring them all together on a virtual battlefield.

470
00:39:47,800 --> 00:39:51,800
In her final resting place, two and a half miles down,

471
00:39:51,801 --> 00:39:56,240
Only a select few will witness the awesome grandeur of Titanic.

472
00:39:57,100 --> 00:40:00,440
But for History Channel competition winner Rob Goldsmith,

473
00:40:00,740 --> 00:40:03,060
a lifetime's dream is about to come true.

474
00:40:03,200 --> 00:40:05,240
I really can't wait. It's just going to be fantastic.

475
00:40:05,900 --> 00:40:08,780
The moving story of that fated voyage

476
00:40:08,781 --> 00:40:11,340
and of one man's chance to live his dream.

477
00:40:11,960 --> 00:40:16,760
Titanic, a tale of two journeys, Friday at 8.30 on the History Channel.

478
00:40:51,800 --> 00:41:04,580
The Armoured Training Facility at Fort Knox, Kentucky, is where the US Army trains soldiers

479
00:41:04,581 --> 00:41:08,780
how to operate tanks.

480
00:41:08,781 --> 00:41:14,680
In the 1980s, it housed a room full of simulators used by tank crews to hone their driving and

481
00:41:14,681 --> 00:41:16,440
firing skills.

482
00:41:16,441 --> 00:41:20,220
High-tech as it was, it still had limitations.

483
00:41:20,221 --> 00:41:25,760
The limitations of these class of simulators in the early 80s was that it was focused only

484
00:41:25,761 --> 00:41:33,000
on a single person or a single crew, but warfighting skills require more than just individual tasks.

485
00:41:33,001 --> 00:41:37,340
It requires the training and proficiencies of the crews, the crews and battoons, and

486
00:41:37,341 --> 00:41:41,740
the interaction of those battoons and companies and battalions.

487
00:41:41,741 --> 00:41:47,180
That required somehow connecting simulators together onto a common playing field, a challenge

488
00:41:47,181 --> 00:41:52,180
taken up by DARPA, the advanced research arm of the Department of Defense.

489
00:41:52,181 --> 00:41:57,180
Designers there soon realized that the problem wasn't merely a technical one.

490
00:41:57,181 --> 00:42:04,180
Many of the companies that were building the simulators of those day built stand-alone simulation.

491
00:42:04,181 --> 00:42:08,180
And they were quite comfortable with selling the government their product.

492
00:42:08,181 --> 00:42:15,180
And the idea of coming up with network simulation was seen as a challenge to their marketing strategy.

493
00:42:15,181 --> 00:42:22,180
Despite this resistance, DARPA came up with SimNet, a way to connect rival simulators.

494
00:42:22,181 --> 00:42:29,180
Its first real test came in November 1992 at a simulator hardware convention in San Antonio, Texas.

495
00:42:29,181 --> 00:42:35,180
DARPA invited all the participants, competitors whose hardware was never designed to operate in unison,

496
00:42:35,181 --> 00:42:38,180
to connect up using SimNet.

497
00:42:38,181 --> 00:42:41,180
There was a great deal of nervous about taking a new technology,

498
00:42:41,181 --> 00:42:46,560
technology, taking people that had no real experience but understood the theory and had

499
00:42:46,561 --> 00:42:51,820
read the books, but to ask them to come to a location to plug into somebody else's network,

500
00:42:51,821 --> 00:42:55,340
to interact with other companies that they had never interacted with, was a great deal

501
00:42:55,341 --> 00:42:56,041
of risk.

502
00:42:56,042 --> 00:43:00,420
Ensuring that the wires were laid, that nobody cut the wires, that they all ran

503
00:43:00,421 --> 00:43:04,740
to the right place, is just a Herculean task to kind of get everybody connected in a very,

504
00:43:04,741 --> 00:43:07,060
very short period of time.

505
00:43:07,061 --> 00:43:13,880
Sunday night before the demonstration, I think five out of the eight legs were either run over by heavy equipment or cut by a carpet cutter.

506
00:43:14,240 --> 00:43:19,440
It was now dark. The stores are closed and time to fix it.

507
00:43:19,540 --> 00:43:22,760
We got the best and brightest here. I can't believe the answer is no.

508
00:43:23,140 --> 00:43:28,080
I think we got the last piece of the network screwed together at five minutes of one o'clock in the morning.

509
00:43:28,081 --> 00:43:29,840
I sure wish we had had a rehearsal.

510
00:43:31,420 --> 00:43:32,420
Damn, it worked.

511
00:43:33,020 --> 00:43:34,320
It was a miracle, really.

512
00:43:34,321 --> 00:43:37,220
We've let the genie out of the bottle.

513
00:43:37,221 --> 00:43:39,220
The future will never be the same.

514
00:43:39,221 --> 00:43:42,520
It ran and ran extremely well.

515
00:43:42,521 --> 00:43:48,140
It ran beyond all of our wildest expectations that it was going to be as successful as it

516
00:43:48,141 --> 00:43:49,140
was.

517
00:43:49,141 --> 00:43:53,160
You would see a tank and a Bradley next to each other.

518
00:43:53,161 --> 00:43:58,580
Then you could walk down the conference hall and stop in at the booth where someone was

519
00:43:58,581 --> 00:44:02,720
generating the tank, see exactly what it looked like from their viewpoint.

520
00:44:02,721 --> 00:44:15,260
The tank platoons that fought in the Gulf War were trained to coordinate their attacks on network simulators.

521
00:44:17,140 --> 00:44:22,480
At the end of the war, one of its battles was precisely recreated on a simulator database,

522
00:44:22,960 --> 00:44:25,520
becoming the first example of virtual history.

523
00:44:25,521 --> 00:44:29,380
It became a history lesson of what occurred.

524
00:44:29,381 --> 00:44:34,700
But more importantly, people wanted to be able to think about what if I went back and

525
00:44:34,701 --> 00:44:40,120
changed some of the parameters, what would the battle outcome have been?

526
00:44:40,121 --> 00:44:46,620
This networking technology has evolved into CCTT, Close Combat Tactical Trainer.

527
00:44:46,621 --> 00:44:52,180
500 individual simulators representing many types of armored vehicles can be linked together

528
00:44:52,181 --> 00:44:54,380
on a common virtual battlefield.

529
00:44:54,381 --> 00:45:03,520
When you get inside of a CCD simulator, it looks just like the inside of a tank or just like the inside of a Bradley.

530
00:45:03,521 --> 00:45:09,160
You could take an armor company from a Fort Knox, tie it together with an infantry company at Fort Benning,

531
00:45:09,440 --> 00:45:15,360
put them on the ground in this very large 3D representation of a piece of terrain that was actually in Germany.

532
00:45:18,120 --> 00:45:19,700
Fire, fire, heat adjust.

533
00:45:19,701 --> 00:45:24,700
But soldiers aren't the only ones visiting virtual worlds.

534
00:45:24,701 --> 00:45:28,700
This isn't a roller coaster, it's a simulator.

535
00:45:28,701 --> 00:45:34,560
In the mid-80s, simulators began to be used for an entirely new purpose.

536
00:45:34,561 --> 00:45:35,700
Entertainment.

537
00:45:39,700 --> 00:45:45,700
The world's first virtual ride, Tour of the Universe, opened in Toronto in 1984,

538
00:45:45,701 --> 00:45:49,300
built out of a modified 747 aircraft simulator.

539
00:45:52,100 --> 00:45:56,100
Within a few years, smaller versions began popping up everywhere,

540
00:45:56,300 --> 00:45:59,100
giving audiences a chance to experience the thrill

541
00:45:59,101 --> 00:46:02,300
of both the chillingly real and the fantastic.

542
00:46:10,700 --> 00:46:13,500
Universal Studios' Back to the Future ride

543
00:46:13,501 --> 00:46:15,500
took things one step further,

544
00:46:15,501 --> 00:46:21,500
projecting visuals onto an enormous dome that put people literally inside the experience.

545
00:46:26,500 --> 00:46:30,500
Simulators also began to make their way onto home computers.

546
00:46:30,501 --> 00:46:35,500
In 1983 Microsoft introduced its first flight simulator,

547
00:46:35,501 --> 00:46:38,500
a simplified version of what the pros used.

548
00:46:38,501 --> 00:46:43,500
Fifteen years later, Microsoft Flight Simulator has become so sophisticated

549
00:46:43,501 --> 00:46:50,500
but the military use a customized version for pilots at the beginning of their training.

550
00:46:55,760 --> 00:47:00,180
Another computer game, SimCity, took a different approach to simulation,

551
00:47:00,181 --> 00:47:04,020
allowing players to lay out and manage their own virtual city,

552
00:47:04,021 --> 00:47:07,760
with simulated citizens that move in or move out

553
00:47:07,761 --> 00:47:12,760
if they're not happy with how you're running things.

554
00:47:13,500 --> 00:47:23,860
Simulators have also returned to arcades, moving beyond early games to incorporate real-life replicas of motorcycles, snowboards, even whitewater rapids.

555
00:47:42,449 --> 00:47:47,240
The future undoubtedly holds new and even broader uses for simulators, including those

556
00:47:47,241 --> 00:47:53,820
designed for battling disasters, testing assembly lines before the factory is even built, drawing

557
00:47:53,821 --> 00:47:58,660
an even finer line between their virtual world and our reality.

